# K6 — count the primes below 10,000,000. Anchor 664579 = pi(10^7).
#
# READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING THE TIMING. This is a DELEGATION kernel. Helix ships the
# Sieve of Eratosthenes as a native builtin (`primes(n)` — the byte-array sieve in
# Rust at src/interp/builtins.rs::sieve_primes), so this line measures ONE thing:
# *does the language ship the right primitive?* It says NOTHING about Helix's codegen
# — it is the same kind of result NumPy gets on a matmul by calling BLAS. The sieve is
# an inherently MUTABLE algorithm that Helix's immutable surface cannot express
# efficiently, which is exactly why it is a builtin.
#
# DO NOT read this as beating C. All four native columns run the same byte-array
# Eratosthenes, so they tie. Measured on one (contended) WSL2 box, N=1e7, min of 25
# full-process runs:
#     C 0.017 s | Helix 0.020 s | Rust 0.020 s | Go 0.023 s
# That spread is the noise floor, not a ranking; ~0.004 s of the Helix number is
# interpreter startup. Where Helix does pay is memory, not time: 44 MB peak RSS
# against C's 11 MB, because `primes()` materializes all 664579 primes as an i64
# array that `.count()` then merely measures, where C counts inline.
#
# The one honest win here is CPython (0.63 s, 31x — best of 6). Helix also "beats"
# NumPy's ~0.08 s total, but that is an artifact of process startup: ~0.065 s of that
# total is `import numpy`, and NumPy's sieve alone is ~0.013 s — the fastest sieve in
# this kernel, since it bounds its outer loop at isqrt(n) and counts vectorized.
#
# The honest pure-Helix count of the same quantity is k6_sieve_trial.helix: 97 s
# against this file's 0.020 s (~4800x, measured). Read BOTH or you have misread this.
#
# `primes(n)` yields the primes strictly BELOW n — the same 2 <= p < n boundary
# every other language in this kernel uses. N is a source literal because `helix run`
# gives a script no argv (house style: see bench/stress/s5_fused.helix). The literal
# is NOT const-folded: the same kernel taking N from read_int() runs at the same speed
# (ratio 1.008, min of 25), so the sieve really executes at run time.
n = read_int() ?? 10000000    # N on stdin; run.sh pipes it
print(primes(n).count())
